# The Chomsky hierarchy, the ladder of language classes ranked by the memory their machines need, with the strict
# ordering machine-checked by `seed test`. Index the levels by memory: a finite automaton has level 0 (no memory beyond
# its state, the regular languages), a pushdown automaton level 1 (a stack, the context-free languages), a
# linear-bounded automaton level 2 (a bounded tape, the context-sensitive languages), and a Turing machine level 3 (an
# unbounded tape, the recursively-enumerable languages). Each level strictly contains the one below, so the indices
# strictly increase.

# Regular sits below context-free: level 0 is below level 1.
hold <regular is below context-free>
  call is-below
    code 0
    code 1

# Context-free sits below context-sensitive: level 1 is below level 2.
hold <context-free is below context-sensitive>
  call is-below
    code 1
    code 2

# Context-sensitive sits below recursively-enumerable: level 2 is below level 3.
hold <context-sensitive is below recursively-enumerable>
  call is-below
    code 2
    code 3

# The ordering is transitive across the whole ladder: regular (0) sits below recursively-enumerable (3).
hold <regular is below recursively-enumerable>
  call is-below
    code 0
    code 3
